<h2 align=center><font color=black>Biographical Sketch</font></h2>
I was born in southern Portugal where I lived until the age of 11. At this stage, my parents emigrated to the Republic of South Africa where we spent the next four years. It was here that I picked up passions for tennis and rugby, while struggling to learn the other official language, Afrikaans. 

In the 1980s, my parents having returned to Portugal, I continued my education in England (that "green and pleasant land"), first at a private boarding school (Sutton Valence, Kent), and later at the University of Southampton where I completed a B.Sc. in Mathematics in 1988.

Wanting to pursue a Ph.D. in America, I gained admission to the University of Oklahoma (OU), where I finally arrived in 1990, after a "gap-year" spent exploring various parts of the world (Europe, North Africa, South America). I graduated in 1992 with an M.A. in Mathematics. Very significantly, OU was also the place where I met my future wife, and saw my first tornado!

Disenchanted with a career in mathematics (and its job prospects), I spent 1993-1995 working as a programmer/analyst with the IBM Corporation (Irving, Texas), in the capacity of an outside consultant. It was here that I regrouped and began to think about statistics as a possible long-term career path.

Not knowing whether I would really like the subject, in 1995 I applied to graduate school once again, and was accepted at Colorado State University (CSU) to pursue a Ph.D. in Statistics. I soon discovered that this was the right field for me. Not only was it interesting, but employment prospects were excellent. These were exciting and formative years; Richard Tweedie ran the Department and (like him) I began to think that all problems could be solved with statistics...

Having (finally) completed my dissertation in the summer of 2000, I defended it on August 1, before rushing to the University of Florida (UF) where I started as Assistant Professor a few weeks later. Very significantly, UF was also the place where I saw my first hurricane!   

